{{!-- Our Commitment to Transparency, the text and the list of machine-
     translated works. One copy, used by the standalone page
     (custom-faith-transparency.hbs) and by the fourth tab of the About
     page (custom-faith-manifesto.hbs, Ian 2026-09-29: "add the
     transparency commitment as another tab"). The list needs
     faith-transparency.js and the three catalogue helpers it reads
     (faith-denominations, faith-corpora, faith-century) on the page;
     the Report button needs faith-report-issue.js. --}}
      <p class="faith-tp-lede">We understand the gravity of a project like The Faith Received. It is no light thing to publish nearly 20,000 primary sources of the Christian faith in one place, around 15,000 of which were translated into English by a machine, many of them for the first time. A project of this scope and scale never would have been possible without the help of artificial intelligence.</p>

      <p>But we are not ignorant of the promises and the perils of using this technology in this way. It matters that the quality of these translations is good, not that they simply exist. Which is why we do not see The Faith Received as a static resource, but as a living project, and one that requires radical transparency and openness to iteration.</p>

      <p>With that in mind, we have undertaken eight steps to ensure transparency and ongoing improvement of quality.</p>

      {{!-- Numbered as an ordered list rather than as prose with
            digits in it, so a screen reader announces eight steps and
            says which one it is on. --}}
      <ol class="faith-tp-steps">
        <li>
          <h2>Everything translated by artificial intelligence says so</h2>
          <p>Every machine-translated work carries a Translation Transparency panel at the top of the page. It names the language the text was translated from, before the text rather than in a footnote after it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>The original is always beside the translation</h2>
          <p>Every machine-translated work carries the English, the original language, and, where the source digitised one, the page scan the original was read from. All three are in the Text Tools menu on every work, so anyone can check a sentence against the page it came from.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>Modernized English is a reading aid, not a rewrite</h2>
          <p>The Modernize button in Tools updates older English as you read. Thou becomes you, hath becomes has, and connexion becomes connection. It follows fixed rules and a checked word list. It does not use artificial intelligence. It never touches the Latin or Greek, and turning it off restores every word as printed.</p>
          <p>Its spelling list draws on MorphAdorner, developed by Academic and Research Technologies at Northwestern University, and is used under its license.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>Anyone can report a problem</h2>
          <p>Every work has a Report An Issue button in the same menu. Reports go to an inbox that is reviewed. Works are updated as deemed appropriate.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>The reports are counted in public</h2>
          <p>Each work shows how many issues have been reported against it and how many have been settled.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>Every correction is dated and published</h2>
          <p>When a work is corrected, what changed is written into that work's own history with the date. A translation that changes silently is worse than one that was wrong, because a reader who checked it once has no way to know it moved.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>A translation committee is being formed</h2>
          <p>We are assembling credentialed translators to begin checking these works and making the changes their reading calls for.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>Every work says where it stands</h2>
          <p>Works carry a status of Reviewed or Needs Review. Until a translator has read a work, it says Needs Review, because silence has to mean nobody has checked this and never that it is fine. A reviewed work should be trusted further than one that has not been.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="faith-tp-act">
        {{!-- The library's own button, not the magazine's pill. .tfr-review-btn
            is in the TFR button family (faith-received.css), which is what
            every other control inside The Faith Received wears. --}}
        <button type="button" class="tfr-review-btn" data-report-issue>Report an issue</button>
      </p>

      {{!-- The changelog (Ian, 2026-09-30): every correction made to a work,
